WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 3: Reflections.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:11.420
Unit 3 is called Reflections because it asks you to look
closely at the world and say what you see, think and feel.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.360
You will read a rhyming river poem, three
haiku and a reflective passage about the wind,

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:23.200
and you will meet figurative language:
simile, metaphor and personification.

5
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:31.580
The story 'The Cracked Charter' takes the same skill into
a museum mystery about a town charter with a crack running

6
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.080
through it.

7
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.880
The grammar focus is comparative and superlative
adjectives, which you will use to compare poems,

8
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.580
texts and ideas in your own reflective writing.

9
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.980
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:54.740
By the end of this unit, you will be able to identify and
explain similes, metaphors and personification in poems.

11
00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.480
You will also read and compare different kinds
of poetry, including haiku and rhyming verse.

12
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.220
You will also count syllables and explain
how poets use rhythm, sound and line length.

13
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.640
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:16.120
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: embarrass,
exaggerate, marvellous, mischievous, privilege, conversely.

15
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.820
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.340
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.840
The reading sequence includes The
River and Three Haiku for a Year.

18
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.380
Read actively.

19
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.300
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.220
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.080
The first language focus is What
Comparatives and Superlatives Do.

22
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.340
Comparatives and superlatives are special forms of
adjectives that let you weigh one thing against another.

23
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:58.360
A comparative measures exactly two things; a superlative
ranks one thing against a whole group of three or more.

24
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.500
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

25
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.960
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Choral Verse Performance.

26
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.980
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

27
00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.040
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

28
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.840
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

29
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.480
You are ready to begin.

30
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.480
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

31
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.220
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

32
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:41.720
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
